WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.489
<v Jamie>The Supreme Court just blocked Trump from firing the Federal Reserve chair, Lisa Cook stays put, markets jumped zero point seven four percent, and suddenly there's an actual institutional speed bump on the dashboard.
  ↳ source: https://www.marketwatch.com/story/supreme-court-allows-lisa-cook-to-stay-on-at-fed-for-now-whats-next-as-trump-tries-to-fire-her-06bfb9ee?mod=mw_rss_topstories

00:00:11.489 --> 00:00:35.782
<v Alex>Tech just pulled the whole market forward while healthcare got sandbagged. S&P up zero point seven four percent, but the Nasdaq climbed one point three zero percent—that's the real story. Tesla alone jumped five point two five percent on whatever Elon's having for lunch, Google up four point two eight, Amazon three point seven four. Meanwhile UnitedHealth tanked two point two two percent because apparently keeping people alive is now a policy risk.

00:00:35.782 --> 00:00:47.600
<v Jamie>So the Fed chair stays in her chair, and the market's response is basically to reward the companies that have the most to gain from less regulation and punish the ones that live inside it.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:01:06.968
<v Maya>Right, and the thing that kills me is UnitedHealth's down because investors are suddenly worried about healthcare policy, but Tesla's up five percent because—I don't know, the timeline's good today? Tesla doesn't have a regulatory body breathing down its neck the way health insurers do, so it's just free to exist in the land of whatever happens next.

00:01:06.968 --> 00:01:14.519
<v Jamie>So healthcare gets punished for the same reason tech gets rewarded—which is that everyone just figured out what the rules actually are.

00:01:14.519 --> 00:01:24.367
<v Maya>And Tesla's still just up five percent for being Tesla, which at this point is like being rewarded for showing up to the party with your own gravitational field.

00:01:24.367 --> 00:01:43.079
<v Jamie>So the Supreme Court just said Trump can't fire Lisa Cook unilaterally—but here's what matters: the Court didn't say he can never fire a Fed governor. It said the process has to follow the statute, and the statute says 'for cause.' That's the guardrail. Trump would have to prove incompetence or malfeasance, not just ideological disagreement.
  ↳ source: https://www.marketwatch.com/story/supreme-court-allows-lisa-cook-to-stay-on-at-fed-for-now-whats-next-as-trump-tries-to-fire-her-06bfb9ee?mod=mw_rss_topstories

00:01:43.079 --> 00:02:02.119
<v Alex>Right, and that's massive for the next four years because it means the Fed's leadership doesn't flip the second a new president walks in. Lisa Cook's term runs through twenty twenty-eight. So even if Trump replaces Jerome Powell—which he might try—he can't just hollow out the board with loyalists and turn it into a branch of Treasury.
  ↳ source: https://www.marketwatch.com/story/supreme-court-allows-lisa-cook-to-stay-on-at-fed-for-now-whats-next-as-trump-tries-to-fire-her-06bfb9ee?mod=mw_rss_topstories

00:02:02.119 --> 00:02:20.174
<v Maya>But here's the thing that's actually wild: Trump's own legal team apparently argued that presidents should have absolute removal power over Fed governors, and the Court basically said no, that's not how it works. So he just got schooled by his own lawyers in public, which is the kind of loss that sticks around.

00:02:20.174 --> 00:02:34.947
<v Jamie>The Fed's got twelve governors total, and Cook's one of them. So if Trump wanted to reshape monetary policy, he'd have to wait for seats to open naturally or find a different angle. That's a much slower play than what he was trying to do.

00:02:34.947 --> 00:02:50.704
<v Maya>The other piece nobody's talking about: if Trump can't fire Cook, he also can't fire any Fed governor, which means every regional bank president who might disagree with him is suddenly a lot harder to intimidate. The whole ecosystem just got more resilient to one person's whims.

00:02:50.704 --> 00:03:05.477
<v Jamie>So the market popped zero point seven four percent not because the Fed's suddenly dovish, but because institutional stability just became real again. That's the actual win here—not what the Fed does next, but that nobody gets to unilaterally decide what the Fed does.

00:03:05.477 --> 00:03:15.653
<v Maya>The Supreme Court allowed firing of FTC commissioners, which means whoever controls the White House controls whether tech companies get regulated or just get a friendly email once a year.
  ↳ source: https://www.axios.com/2026/06/29/supreme-court-mail-in-voting-grace-period

00:03:15.653 --> 00:03:23.532
<v Jamie>So the FTC had structural independence, and now it doesn't. That's not a policy shift—that's a staffing choice masquerading as a court decision.

00:03:23.532 --> 00:03:37.320
<v Alex>The FTC's got five commissioners, and the president can now replace them without cause. Lina Khan's term was already precarious; now every tech merger review and privacy enforcement action just became subject to whoever's sitting in the Oval Office that quarter.

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:49.138
<v Maya>Alphabet just joined the Dow Jones—the first time the index has admitted that a company worth two trillion dollars and built on surveillance advertising should probably be in the same basket as Boeing and Coca-Cola.

00:03:49.138 --> 00:04:01.612
<v Alex>The Dow's adding Alphabet and removing Solventum, which is the index's way of saying the economy is tech now—there's no separating it anymore. Alphabet's market cap is bigger than the entire healthcare sector was five years ago.

00:04:01.612 --> 00:04:14.415
<v Jamie>So we've got the FTC losing independence to the White House, and then the Dow adding the company that benefits most from the FTC losing independence. That's not a coincidence—that's the market pricing in the actual new reality.

00:04:14.415 --> 00:04:25.577
<v Maya>Oil's sitting near pre-war lows, but OPEC's holding its breath because the Strait of Hormuz is still a chokepoint and Iran's still got leverage. One naval incident and crude jumps thirty percent overnight.

00:04:25.577 --> 00:04:32.142
<v Alex>Supply risks haven't gone away—they've just been discounted because the market's betting on continued quiet. That's a fragile assumption.

00:04:32.142 --> 00:04:40.021
<v Jamie>So we've got FTC independence gone, tech in the Dow, and oil prices held down by hope that nothing explodes. That's the week.

00:04:40.021 --> 00:05:07.925
<v Maya>Teyana Taylor got the Icon of the Year at the B E T Awards, Janet Jackson handed it to her, and Taylor just completely fell apart—like, genuine tears, the kind you can't fake on a live broadcast. And then instead of a slick performance, she pivoted straight into a Lauryn Hill tribute, which is basically saying 'I'm going to spend my victory lap honoring someone else,' and the internet lost it because it was messy and real and nobody had choreographed it to death.
  ↳ source: https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cpd3y6lv819o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss

00:05:07.925 --> 00:05:19.743
<v Jamie>So she wins the biggest award of her career, and her instinct isn't to milk the moment—it's to cry and then redirect the spotlight to Lauryn Hill. That's not a acceptance speech, that's a confession.

00:05:19.743 --> 00:05:31.889
<v Alex>The B E T Awards spent months building production around this, and the moment that actually resonated was the one nobody rehearsed. Everything else was set pieces; that was just a person realizing what she'd accomplished.

00:05:31.889 --> 00:05:46.990
<v Maya>Exactly—we've been fed so much content that's been edited and filtered and optimized for engagement that watching someone's actual nervous system activate on live television somehow became the most viral thing of the night. The tears were the production. The tribute was the mic drop.

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:53.555
<v Alex>Lisa Cook stays in her chair because the Court decided institutions matter more than whoever's holding the remote control.
  ↳ source: https://www.marketwatch.com/story/supreme-court-allows-lisa-cook-to-stay-on-at-fed-for-now-whats-next-as-trump-tries-to-fire-her-06bfb9ee?mod=mw_rss_topstories

00:05:53.555 --> 00:05:58.480
<v Maya>Which means the Fed's not a vending machine that changes hands every four years.

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:03.075
<v Jamie>This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
